2026-10-23|Maximum K for Sorting a Permutation Using Bitwise AND Swaps

Question:
Given a permutation nums of integers from 0 to n - 1, we may swap elements at indices i and j only if:
(nums[i] AND nums[j]) == k
Return the maximum non-negative k that allows the array to be sorted. If nums is already sorted, return 0.

Approach:
1. If nums is already sorted, return 0.
2. Observe that a swap producing AND value k requires both swapped values to contain every set bit present in k.
3. For any element that is misplaced, it must be possible to move it to its correct position through valid swaps.
4. Because every value belongs to the range [0, n - 1], the maximum possible k cannot exceed the bitwise AND of all misplaced values.
5. Calculate the bitwise AND of every value whose current index differs from its value.
6. Return that AND value. If the result is zero, zero is the only possible candidate.

Solution:
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int sortPermutation(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int k = -1;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (nums[i] != i) {
                k &= nums[i];
            }
        }

        return k == -1 ? 0 : k;
    }
};

Time Complexity: O(n), because we traverse the array once.

Space Complexity: O(1), because only a constant number of variables are used.
